## Purpose

Implements the next independent Wave 2 grain: governed observations for delivery routes and delivery stops.

## Authority boundary

The package deliberately separates evidence classes:

- typed POD rows are durable proof evidence;
- delivery exception rows are durable exception evidence;
- departure acknowledgements are durable pre-route evidence;
- driver-location rows contribute counts and event coverage only; coordinates are never copied;
- `ecoflow_day_state` route and stop payloads are current-state observations and are not claimed as a complete event ledger;
- notifications are communication evidence only and never prove delivery.

## Facts

Adds service-only, RLS-protected:

- `analytics.fact_delivery_route_observation` — one observed version per business day and route instance;
- `analytics.fact_delivery_stop_observation` — one observed version per business day, route and order stop;
- route and stop quality views;
- `analytics.refresh_delivery_route_stop_facts(...)` as the only derived-fact write path.

## Outcome rules

- `DELIVERED` requires current stop state `DELIVERED` plus both `POD1_DROP_POINT` and `POD2_GOODS_PLACED` durable proof rows.
- A delivered state with incomplete proof remains `DELIVERED_UNVERIFIED`.
- Durable exceptions retain authority over notification state.
- Failed, skipped, arrived and pending day-state remains explicitly transitional when no durable terminal evidence exists.
- Sent notifications remain counts only and cannot change the delivery outcome.

## Privacy and history restraint

- no latitude, longitude, POD object path, recipient email or phone is stored in ordinary facts;
- GPS is represented only as source/event counts and first/latest sample timestamps;
- refresh-created versions preserve observed semantic changes, but do not claim every overwritten day-state transition was captured;
- no trigger is installed on any operational table.

## Driver dimension history

The main refresh can observe a later Driver display-name change. A separate analytics-only SCD guard ensures that this does not rewrite history:

- the version boundary uses the Driver source timestamp, not generic database clock-maintained `updated_at`;
- the prior dimension ID is restored and closed;
- a new current Driver dimension is inserted;
- existing Route observations retain the prior Driver dimension ID;
- same-effective-instant corrections remain in place and do not manufacture history;
- runtime roles cannot execute the trigger function directly.

## Deployment restraint

The migrations create schema, quality views, refresh rows and an analytics-only Driver SCD trigger. They do not invoke refresh, backfill production data, activate metrics, or change Driver, route, POD, exception, notification, Returns or GPS writes.

## PostgreSQL contract

The PostgreSQL 16 contracts verify:

1. no automatic population or metric activation;
2. browser denial and service-role read-only facts;
3. route lock/start/end, planned stops and notification counts;
4. two different drivers across departure/GPS resolve as `MULTIPLE`;
5. typed two-photo POD confirms delivery;
6. one-photo delivery remains unverified;
7. durable unassigned refusal remains durable exception evidence;
8. a sent notification does not convert a pending stop to delivered;
9. invalid text business dates do not break refresh or enter facts;
10. source timestamp-only changes do not create a new version;
11. completing the second POD creates a new retained version;
12. a later Driver name change creates a new Driver SCD version;
13. historical Route facts remain bound to the original Driver dimension;
14. a same-effective-instant Driver correction remains in place;
15. all existing Warehouse, Returns, Inventory, Pick, Driver and Notification contracts still pass.

## Scope

Six files only:

- two additive analytics migrations;
- two PostgreSQL behaviour contracts;
- one static boundary audit;
- one CI release-order update.

No frontend or dependency changes.

